<script lang="ts">
	import { examples } from '../../utils/examples'
</script>
<template>
	<DocsPage
		product="Meeko i18n"
		locale="en"
		title="Languages and routes"
		description="Default language without a prefix, generated /en/… routes, localized links, switcher, hreflang and sitemap."
	>
		<h2 id="strategy">Default language without a prefix</h2>
		<p>
			With
			<code>strategy: 'prefix_except_default'</code>
			, the default option, the URLs of the default language do not change and every other language lives under its prefix. The module generates the
			<code>/en/…</code>
			routes from the existing pages, with the same file, layouts and options: there is no need to duplicate pages.
		</p>
		<DocsCode
			label="meeko.config.ts"
			bind:code="examples.strategy"
		/>
		<p>
			A project page at the URL of another language, such as
			<code>app/pages/en/legal.meeko</code>
			, replaces the generated route: that is how the prose of a page is written in each language.
			<code>exclude</code>
			leaves a route without versions and
			<code>partial</code>
			limits the ones of a language with a partial translation. A URL with the prefix of the default language, such as
			<code>/es/guide</code>
			, does not exist.
		</p>
		<h2 id="links">Localized links</h2>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.links" />
		<p>
			<code>localePath</code>
			adds the prefix of the document's language, or of the language it receives, and keeps the search and the fragment. Use it in the internal links of the interface.
		</p>
		<h2 id="switch">Language switcher</h2>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.routing" />
		<p>
			<code>switchLocalePath</code>
			returns the equivalent route of the current page in another language and
			<code>pageLocales</code>
			the languages that have a version of it. Use real links: they work without JavaScript and search engines follow them. Changing the language is a full navigation that stores the choice in a cookie;
			<code>switchLocale</code>
			does the same from a script.
		</p>
		<h2 id="seo">hreflang and sitemap</h2>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.head" />
		<p>
			Each localized page declares
			<code>lang</code>
			,
			<code>dir</code>
			and one
			<code>link rel="alternate"</code>
			per language plus
			<code>x-default</code>
			, with absolute URLs based on
			<code>site</code>
			. With
			<code>sitemap: true</code>
			, Meeko's
			<code>sitemap.xml</code>
			lists each version with its alternates.
		</p>
		<h2 id="suggestion">Suggest the language, without redirecting</h2>
		<DocsCode bind:code="examples.suggestion" />
		<p>
			URLs without a prefix always show the default language: the module never redirects based on
			<code>Accept-Language</code>
			or the cookie. That way each URL has a single content that search engines can index and caches can share.
			<code>suggestedLocale</code>
			proposes, only in the browser, the language of the cookie or of
			<code>navigator.languages</code>
			so that you can show a notice.
		</p>
		<h2 id="helpers">URL utilities</h2>
		<DocsCode
			label="Explicit imports"
			bind:code="examples.paths"
		/>
		<p>
			<code>localeOf</code>
			returns the language of the first segment or the fallback it receives.
			<code>localizedPath</code>
			replaces a known prefix and omits the one of the default language;
			<code>negotiateLocale</code>
			chooses a configured language from an
			<code>Accept-Language</code>
			header or from a list of preferences.
		</p>
		<UILink href="/en/playground?tab=demo#messages">Try a switch with a query and a fragment</UILink>
	</DocsPage>
</template>
